when I first said I was getting indie  sports a lot of the people I spoke with  were former professional owners of  franchises and traditional sports former  teammates I remember telling Shaq I told  Kobe they didn't understand what it was  but within a year Shaq became part of an  eSport organization Kobe is exploring it  we have a lot of traditional athletes  that actually their own or have invested  in eSports teams  [Music]  for as long as videogames have existed  there's been a competitive gaming scene  but if you recognize the potential of  gaming as a spectator sport it's only  over the last decade with the rise of  streaming technology an influx of  investment that eSports have been able  to grow to what they are today many of  these investors come from an unlikely  place the world of traditional sports  today we're talking a former basketball  pro Rick Fox owner of the team 100  thieves nadeshot  and the assistant manager of the Golden  State Warriors Kirk wake up to examine  the connection between esports and  traditional sports organizations like  the NBA so coming from the NBA for  myself personally having been a part of  a competitive environment on a large  scale globally and entering eSports and  having to both you know seeing both  communities as having their own value  and their own expression of competition  I love that I saw in so many ways the  similarities I saw two ecosystems  standing alone and really impacting the  entertainment value for viewer I think  it's really interesting that there have  been so many NBA owners and NBA players  that had invested in eSports  holistically but for me it's actually  much broader than that there was a  stigma or a stereotype behind gaming for  a very long time that's you're in your  mom's basement Dorito crumbs Mountain  Dew all day every day and that's what  gaming was but now what people don't  realize or what they're quickly  realizing is that video games are pop  culture now all of our athletes and  artists I have that are now coming into  popularity and building their careers  have started with video games they grew  up with that personally I've been a  video game player since I was a teenager  so the understanding that the games that  I love and play had evolved to a place  where you could compete professionally  and have a career was very interesting  to me because it represented the next  generation of professional athletes and  gamers I work for the Warriors in  basketball and my job spans a lot of  things it's fans the actual basketball  side it spans business it spans kind of  new ventures and we are looking for  things that really had a parallel to  what we were doing we're an  entertainment company  and gaming is huge League of Legends is  huge  I am the VP of eSports for clutch gaming  and the Houston Rockets the thing that  really wakes me up and gets me to sleep  it in the day it's a sustainability of  our organization and the long-term  growth of the organization we all want  to be just a flash in the pan with the  influence of traditional sports  organizations and the establishment of  franchise teams the infrastructure  around LCS is evolving but what goes  into running an eSports organization how  is the support effecting the LCS and how  are the conditions changing for players  as the 2018 season the sort of format  for picking teams changed from a  promotion and relegation system where  teams have come from the lower leagues  and take your spot to one a franchising  where teams actually bought in bought  their spots and could hold on to it for  several years or or forever based on how  things go  historically a long time ago when you  saw eSports teams come to fruition they  weren't making a ton of money and now  that you have capital being invested  into the teams you have a lot more  operational ability to go out and sign  better players and work on stronger  branded deals with non-endemic sponsors  that are coming into this space with the  backing of the Golden State Warriors we  have so many resources at our disposal  we're able to think long term about  things we're able to think about  building something that can last this  includes things like building out a  practice facility making sure our  players are housed supporting them with  coaches and having general managers that  are paying attention to their travel and  then getting them into the competition's  they need to be in the vast majority of  costs it with regards to an eSports  organization staff right and that's just  true and I think most industries but  particularly in eSports players are  expensive and it's and they deserve to  be expensive it's it's a free market  there they're working really hard and  they should make them levy to all the  NBA investors that are investing in the  LCS definitely helps LCS become  sustainable since before we didn't  really have that good of a salary and it  was more beneficial to stream compared  to being salaried as a player but now  that all the investors are here  definitely helps a lot by being able to  just focus on the competitive aspect and  then on top of that the operational  costs social media of the type of  content we're creating the video content  that stuff needs to level up over time  and that requires more investment into  cameras  people he sports is 24/7 365 days and we  are building an industry and building on  an industry foundation that's been laid  long before I showed up but what what  I'm hoping to do is add value to this  community of eSports by delivering on  the same passion the same energy and  there is no there's no off days so now  that we've identified the similarities  between eSports and traditional sports  the connection is a whole lot more clear  but what are those who are investing all  their time energy and money see in the  future the first thing I think I'd love  to see it's just much more understanding  among the greater world of the different  opportunities in eSports  we need accountants we need graphics  designers we need musicians we need  people who can you know run our PR run  our social media we could use everyone  and we can just really use good people  the future of Reid sports for me is  really important just because it is  giving me an opportunity to build a  career around something I'd love to do  and of course I want to build a great  financial business so it's really really  important for me is to be able to help  young kids who were in the same position  that I was continue to do what they love  to do I grew up with the stigmas around  video games and I hated them and so you  know my journey is simply just to  continue to shine a positive light on  the impact of what what this industry is  doing for my son what it's doing for  other kids what it's done for me in my  life in retirement as an NBA player  we're not just putting in money but  we're putting in time we're putting in  man-hours and we're putting in like our  own sweat and blood and ultimately it's  gonna be worth it  I truly believe eSports would be a  top-three sport in the world in the next  five to ten years every large gaming  event has at least one thing in common  cosplayers in the next episode of this  series we talked to some incredible  cosplayers about what they love about  their craft and dive into their  workshops to see how they become our  favorite characters  [Music]  you

